{"summary":"Accepted the deserialization-compatibility gap, the stale-cache TDS/conductivity divergence, and the NH3 labeling inconsistency. Rejected the unused-conversions complaint and the GH precision-change note because they do not establish concrete correctness defects in the current code.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-4-c9fd9fff","decision":"reject","rationale":"`pub mod conversions` already exposes the module as a public library API, so the lack of in-repo call sites does not by itself make it a defect. The module also already matches the documented 'single conversion module' policy; adding `pub use conversions::*` would not address a concrete correctness or compatibility problem."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-b753cd38","decision":"reject","rationale":"This is a very small precision refinement of a derived GH formula, not a serialized-schema change. Saves still persist the underlying Ca/Mg totals rather than GH itself, so the current same-version save/load exactness contract is unaffected; extra documentation or a tolerance test could be nice hardening, but the amendment does not identify a substantive correctness or safety bug."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-2b018d6f","decision":"accept","rationale":"The same NH3-N quantity is labeled `mg N/L` in one TUI screen and `mg NH3-N/L` in another, which is avoidably confusing. `docs/UNITS.md` defines the canonical public label as `mg NH3-N/L`, so standardizing the screens is the correct maintainability and UX fix."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-84ddc83e","decision":"accept","rationale":"The legacy alias table lives in `tank_core`, but `TankSnapshot` deserialization itself does not honor those aliases; only the TUI client normalizes them. That means legacy-only snapshot/load payloads still fail for any other Rust consumer deserializing directly into `tank_core::TankSnapshot`, so the compatibility shim belongs in the core type via `serde(alias)` or equivalent shared logic."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-83b30b59","decision":"accept","rationale":"The code now has two public ways to compute TDS/conductivity for the same `TankState`, and they can disagree because snapshot uses a fresh carbonate projection while the state helpers read cached `bicarbonate_mg_total`. The added test explicitly locks in that divergence, which makes this a real correctness and maintenance hazard that should be unified behind shared logic or synchronized cache updates."}]}